-- want-init.txt --
Create starter support files in the global config directory.

Usage:
  invox init
  invox help init

Behavior:
  Creates the global config directory if it does not exist yet.
  Writes starter versions of config.yaml, customers.yaml, issuer.yaml,
  invoice_defaults.yaml, and template.tex.
  Existing non-empty files are left unchanged.

Config directory:
  $WORK/home/.config/invox

Examples:
  invox init
-- want-template-list.txt --
List available LaTeX invoice templates from the same directory as the resolved default template.

Usage:
  invox template list [--names]

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  --names                 Print only template names

Output:
  Default: NAME<TAB>ABSOLUTE_PATH
  --names: TEMPLATE_NAME per line
  On a terminal, aligned columns under a header. Piped, \, tab, CR and LF in a field are written as \\, \t, \r and \n.

Lookup:
  The directory is derived from the resolved default template path.
  Name-only -t/--template values are resolved in that same directory.

Examples:
  invox template list
  invox template list --names
  invox build invoice.yaml -t multi_vat.tex
-- want-completion.txt --
Generate shell completion scripts.

Usage:
  invox completion zsh

Supported shells:
  zsh

Notes:
  The generated Zsh completion script completes subcommands and template names for render/build.
  Template-name completion is backed by `invox template list --names`.

Quick start:
  source <(invox completion zsh)

Persistent Zsh install:
  mkdir -p ~/.zsh/completions
  invox completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_invox
  Add this before compinit in ~/.zshrc:
    fpath=(~/.zsh/completions $fpath)
    autoload -Uz compinit
    compinit

Example:
  invox completion zsh
-- want-new.txt --
Create a new invoice YAML file with a generated number and prefilled defaults.

Usage:
  invox new CUSTOMER_ID [-o OUTPUT.yaml] [-s SOURCE.yaml] [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml] [-u ISSUER.yaml] [-e] [--from-last]

Required inputs:
  CUSTOMER_ID                  Required positional argument

Default output:
  <invoice.number>.yaml in the current directory

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml
  -u, --issuer PATH       Path to issuer.yaml
  -s, --source PATH       Path to invoice_defaults.yaml
  -o, --output PATH       Output file path (must end with .yaml)
  --from-last             Use the latest archived invoice for CUSTOMER_ID as the source document
  -e, --edit              Open the created invoice in your editor

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`
  issuer.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/issuer.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help issuer`
  invoice_defaults.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/invoice_defaults.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help defaults`
  archive.dir: config.yaml, then $DATA_HOME/invox/invoices

Examples:
  invox new CUST-001
  invox new CUST-001 -e
  invox new CUST-001 --from-last
  invox new CUST-001 -o invoices/2026-0022.yaml -s invoice_defaults.yaml -c customers.yaml -u issuer.yaml
-- want-increment.txt --
Increment the invoice number in an existing invoice YAML file.

Usage:
  invox increment -i INVOICE.yaml [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml]

Required inputs:
  -i, --input PATH        Path to the invoice YAML file

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`

Examples:
  invox increment -i invoice.yaml
  invox increment -i invoices/2026-0022.yaml -c customers.yaml
-- want-validate.txt --
Validate invoice YAML against customers and issuer data.

Usage:
  invox validate -i INVOICE.yaml [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml] [-u ISSUER.yaml]

Required inputs:
  -i, --input PATH        Path to the invoice YAML file

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml
  -u, --issuer PATH       Path to issuer.yaml

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`
  issuer.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/issuer.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help issuer`

Examples:
  invox validate -i invoice.yaml
  invox validate -i invoices/2026-0021.yaml -c customers.yaml -u issuer.yaml
-- want-render.txt --
Render a LaTeX invoice file from YAML data.

Usage:
  invox render -i INVOICE.yaml [-o OUTPUT.tex] [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml] [-u ISSUER.yaml] [-t TEMPLATE.tex]

Required inputs:
  -i, --input PATH        Path to the invoice YAML file

Default output:
  invoice.tex in the current directory

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml
  -u, --issuer PATH       Path to issuer.yaml
  -o, --output PATH       Output file path (must end with .tex)
  -t, --template PATH     Path to invoice_template.tex

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`
  issuer.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/issuer.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help issuer`
  template.tex: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/template.tex

Examples:
  invox render -i invoice.yaml
  invox render -i invoices/2026-0021.yaml -o out/2026-0021.tex -c customers.yaml -u issuer.yaml -t invoice_template.tex
-- want-email.txt --
Create an email draft and open it in the default mail app.

Usage:
  invox email (INVOICE.yaml | INVOICE.pdf | -i INPUT) [-p INVOICE.pdf] [-o OUTPUT.eml [--force]] [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml] [-u ISSUER.yaml] [--to EMAIL] [--subject TEXT]

Required inputs:
  INVOICE.yaml, INVOICE.pdf, or -i, --input PATH  Path to the invoice YAML or built PDF file

Default output:
  <input name>.eml in a new temporary directory, removed after 24 hours

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml
  -u, --issuer PATH       Path to issuer.yaml
  -p, --pdf PATH          Path to the invoice PDF
  -o, --output PATH       Output file path (must end with .eml)
  --to EMAIL              Recipient email override
  --subject TEXT          Email subject override, supports placeholders
  --force                 Overwrite an existing output file

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`
  issuer.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/issuer.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help issuer`
  invoice PDF: input path with .pdf extension by default, or the input itself when the input is a PDF

Behavior:
  Accepts either the invoice YAML file or the built PDF as input.
  When the input is a PDF, the matching YAML file is resolved from the same basename.
  The PDF lookup checks next to the PDF first, then archive.dir.
  Requires invoice.status to be built or archived and the PDF attachment to exist.
  On macOS, opens an editable compose window in Apple Mail with the PDF attached.
  If -o is set, or on non-macOS platforms, writes a .eml draft file and opens it.
  Without -o, the draft is written to a new temporary directory. A later run removes it after 24 hours.
  With -o, the draft is kept. An existing -o file is not replaced unless --force is set.
  Does not send the email and does not change invoice.status.

Examples:
  invox email invoice.yaml
  invox email invoice.pdf
  invox email invoice.yaml --to billing@example.com
  invox email invoices/2026-0021.yaml -p out/2026-0021.pdf -o drafts/2026-0021.eml -c customers.yaml -u issuer.yaml
-- want-build.txt --
Render and compile an invoice PDF with Tectonic.

Usage:
  invox build (INVOICE.yaml | -i INVOICE.yaml) [-o OUTPUT.pdf] [-c CUSTOMERS.yaml] [-u ISSUER.yaml] [-t TEMPLATE.tex] [--archive [--yes]]

Required inputs:
  INVOICE.yaml or -i, --input PATH  Path to the invoice YAML file

Default output:
  the input path with .pdf extension

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  -c, --customers PATH    Path to customers.yaml
  -u, --issuer PATH       Path to issuer.yaml
  -o, --output PATH       Output file path (must end with .pdf)
  --archive               Archive the invoice after a successful PDF build
  --yes                   Replace an archived invoice without asking (required without a terminal)
  -t, --template PATH     Path to invoice_template.tex

Default lookup:
  customers.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/customers.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help customers`
  issuer.yaml: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/issuer.yaml
  schema/docs: run `invox help issuer`
  template.tex: upward project search, then $WORK/home/.config/invox/template.tex

Replacing an archived invoice:
  An invoice with invoice.status archived keeps that status when its PDF is rebuilt.
  With --archive, a working copy from `invox archive edit` replaces the archived invoice it came from.
  On a terminal you are asked to confirm; otherwise pass --yes. Declining exits with status 2.
  The previous version is kept as archive.dir/.history/<path>.<UTC timestamp>.<ext>,
  which archive list, numbering and the duplicate-number check ignore.
  --yes only answers the question; every other check still applies.

Examples:
  invox build invoice.yaml
  invox build invoice.yaml --archive
  invox build invoices/2026-0021.yaml -o out/2026-0021.pdf -c customers.yaml -u issuer.yaml -t invoice_template.tex
-- want-archive.txt --
Archive a built or edited invoice YAML file into the configured archive directory.

Usage:
  invox archive (INVOICE.yaml | -i INVOICE.yaml) [--yes]

Required inputs:
  INVOICE.yaml or -i, --input PATH  Path to the invoice YAML file

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page
  --yes                   Replace an archived invoice without asking (required without a terminal)

Default lookup:
  archive.dir: config.yaml, then $DATA_HOME/invox/invoices

Replacing an archived invoice:
  Archiving a working copy from `invox archive edit` replaces the archived invoice it came from.
  On a terminal you are asked to confirm; otherwise pass --yes. Declining exits with status 2.
  The previous version is kept as archive.dir/.history/<path>.<UTC timestamp>.<ext>,
  which archive list, numbering and the duplicate-number check ignore.
  --yes only answers the question; every other check still applies.

Examples:
  invox archive invoice.yaml
  invox archive invoices/2026-0021.yaml
  invox archive 2026-03-06.yaml --yes
-- want-archive-edit.txt --
Copy an archived invoice into the current directory and mark it as editing.

Usage:
  invox archive edit FILENAME

Required inputs:
  FILENAME                  Required positional argument

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page

Default lookup:
  archive.dir: config.yaml, then $DATA_HOME/invox/invoices

Behavior:
  Copies the archived invoice from archive.dir into the current directory.
  The working copy is written as YAML with invoice.status set to editing.
  Re-running invox archive on that working copy replaces the archived invoice.
  It asks first, or needs --yes without a terminal, and keeps the previous version in archive.dir/.history.

Examples:
  invox archive edit 2026-03-06.yaml
  invox archive edit customer-a/2026-03-06.yaml
-- want-archive-list.txt --
List archived invoices from the configured archive directory.

Usage:
  invox archive list

Required inputs:

Optional flags:
  -h, --help              Show this help page

Default lookup:
  archive.dir: config.yaml, then $DATA_HOME/invox/invoices

Output:
  One archived invoice per line as FILENAME<TAB>CUSTOMER_ID<TAB>ISSUE_DATE<TAB>STATUS
  On a terminal, aligned columns under a header. Piped, \, tab, CR and LF in a field are written as \\, \t, \r and \n.

Examples:
  invox archive list
-- want-version.txt --
Show the invox version.

Usage:
  invox version
  invox --version
